  "question": {
    "text": "Letf $: \\mathbb{R} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$, be twice continuously differentiable (or $f$ \" exists and is continuous) such that $f(0)=f(1)=f^{\\prime}(0)=0$. Then",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "WkVvD",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{f}^{\\prime \\prime}(\\mathrm{c})=0$ for some $\\mathrm{c} \\in \\mathbb{R}$",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "eqopi",
      "text": "there is no point for which $f^{\\prime \\prime}(x)=0$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "q4s6W",
      "text": "at all points $f^{\\prime \\prime}(x)>0$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "oday5",
      "text": "at all points $f^{\\prime \\prime}(x)    < 0$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Hint: \r\n$f(0)=f(1)=0$\r\nBy Rolle's theorem $\\mathrm{f}^{\\prime}(\\mathrm{c})=0$ for some $\\mathrm{c} \\in(0,1)$\r\nNow, $\\mathrm{f}^{\\prime}(0)=\\mathrm{f}^{\\prime}(\\mathrm{c})=0$. Again by Rolle's theorem $\\mathrm{f}^{\\prime \\prime}\\left(\\mathrm{c}_{1}\\right)=0 \\quad \\mathrm{c}_{1} \\in(0, \\mathrm{c})$",
    "image": null
  },
